Solver (warlock):
You didn’t sell your soul or your child for power; you signed a W—9 and shook hands with a man in a suit. Whereas other warlocks experience the thrill and terror of cosmic horror, you instead face the existential dread of corporate mundanity. Look on the bright side, those other chumps aren’t getting free dental.
​
1st Level: Otherworldly Patron(s)
You’ve signed a contract with Valhalla incorporated™, a primarily Gracilian and human PMC, consisting of corporate-hired enforcement agents. Which is why you joined the party. For some reason, their work, and mission are considered advantageous for you to join, and work with your DM to decide the missions you fulfill along the way, as well as the money you are paid for it.
​
1st level: Expanded spell list
These are the standard corporate-mandated, arcane tools that were put at your disposal for the purpose of your job.
​
1st: shield, alarm.
​
2nd: warding wind, magic weapon.
​
3rd: haste, glyph of warding.
​
4th: fabricate, greater invisibility.
​
5th:  creation, cone of cold.
​
1st Level: Company Budget:
Starting at 1st level, you can use your action to request nonmagical weapons, items, and food to your specific location, these are purchased with a budget the company has allotted you. The company budget is equal to your level plus your proficiency, and it resets either on a long rest. These items are corporate property, and except food, losing them will lead to you needing to pay the exact cost of the item. 
These items are magically transported and must be used for specific short term purposes, and returned when that purpose is served via that same magical teleportation.
They’ll know if you try to sell the food.
​
6th level: Corporate Investment 
Starting at 6th level, you can expend a spellslot to invest in the corporate rewards dice table:
Upon receiving the reward, you may use a standard action to activate it whenever you wish.
Using a spellslot above 1st level will grant a bonus d4 you can roll alongside the base dice for the final result; if the final result is higher than the dice table, it automatically defaults to the highest number. Does the corporation structure it this way to squeeze its employees for all they've got? Legally, they are required to say no.
On the bright side, most of these last till you decide to try to get a new one.

